All use cases look good. I'll proceed with the plan.

Wait, I should check `AdminController.ts` again to make sure I don't break anything.
The `getGenerationLogs` method had some lazy loading logic:
```typescript
        if (!container.isRegistered(IGenerationLogRepositoryToken)) {
            const { FirebaseGenerationLogRepository } = require("@/modules/ai/infrastructure/repositories/FirebaseGenerationLogRepository");
            container.register(IGenerationLogRepositoryToken, { useClass: FirebaseGenerationLogRepository });
        }
```
This is because `container.ts` might not have registered these yet if it's running in a context where they weren't explicitly required. However, `container.ts` DOES have these registrations inside the `typeof window === 'undefined'` block.

```typescript
  if (typeof window === 'undefined') {
    // ...
    const { FirebaseGenerationLogRepository } = require("@/modules/ai/infrastructure/repositories/FirebaseGenerationLogRepository");
    // ...
    container.register(IGenerationLogRepositoryToken, {
      useClass: FirebaseGenerationLogRepository
    }, { lifecycle: Lifecycle.Singleton });
    // ...
  }
```

So as long as `AdminController` is resolved from the container in a server context, it should be fine. API routes always run in a server context.

I'll check `src/modules/admin/application/use-cases/GetDashboardStatsUseCase.ts` to see if it's also properly injected.

Yes, it's also properly injected.

Wait, the `AdminController` constructor doesn't have `@inject` for its use case params because use cases are themselves `@injectable()` and don't need tokens (they are concrete classes).

```typescript
    constructor(
        private getDashboardStatsUseCase: GetDashboardStatsUseCase,
        private getFeaturesUseCase: GetFeaturesUseCase,
        private updateFeatureUseCase: UpdateFeatureUseCase,
        private getSettingsUseCase: GetSettingsUseCase,
        private updateSettingsUseCase: UpdateSettingsUseCase
    ) {}
```
This is fine. I'll add `GetGenerationLogsUseCase` here.

I'll double check if `GetGenerationLogsUseCase` has any issues being resolved.
The use case constructor:
```typescript
    constructor(
        @inject(IGenerationLogRepositoryToken)
        private logRepository: IGenerationLogRepository,
        @inject(IUserRepositoryToken)
        private userRepository: IUserRepository
    ) {}
```
Both `IGenerationLogRepositoryToken` and `IUserRepositoryToken` are registered in `container.ts` under the `typeof window === 'undefined'` guard.

I'm ready.

